## Hull Geometry — Recursive Phi Lattice

Mark I applied phi once, globally, as a length-beam-height proportioning rule. Mark II treats phi as a recursive generative function: the hull is a nested sequence of **seven golden-ratio shells**, each scaled down from the last by exactly 1/φ³, embedded inside one another like a structural fugue.

- **Shell 1** (outer envelope): 847m baseline length, retained from Mark I
- **Shell 7** (innermost): a 3.1-meter resonance chamber at the golden center of the vessel's internal volume — 0.618 of the way along the long axis, not the geometric center. Position is non-negotiable: moved to true center, the resonance cascade desyncs by 4.7%, audible at altitude as a low groan in the frame.
- Each shell rotates its major axis **36° relative to the shell containing it** (the pentagon angle, derived from cos 36° = φ/2). Viewed end-on in cutaway, the seven shells produce a decagram pattern — not decorative. The 36° offset distributes load from any external stress vector across all seven shells simultaneously rather than concentrating it on the outer hull, absorbing force the way a planetary shield absorbs radiation, nested instead of radial.

