## Lift System — Gradient Buoyancy Architecture

Forty-seven helium cells remain, plus a new gradient buoyancy spine: a central phi-tapered ballast column running the full 847m length, partitioned into 21 segments with individually modulated helium purity (94%–99.7%). Varying purity segment-by-segment shifts the vessel's center of buoyancy fore-aft and port-starboard without a single moving control surface — trim through gas chemistry instead of mechanics. Full nose-up to full nose-down response: 94 seconds — slow, but zero drag cost and nothing mechanical to fail.

Emergency buoyancy recovery upgrades from electrolysis-on-demand to a standing reserve: four segments held sealed in reserve, releasable in under 11 seconds if primary cells are breached. The ship can lose up to 30% of primary lift and still maintain controlled descent rather than freefall.

